---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Sidebar from '../components/Sidebar.astro';
---

<Base title="About" description="About MedWiki.">
  <div class="page">
    <Sidebar />

    <main class="main" id="main">
      <h1 class="page-title">About MedWiki</h1>

      <p class="lead">
        MedWiki is a free, open-access medical encyclopedia written in plain language for
        patients, students and curious readers.
      </p>

      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        MedWiki is a small, static, encyclopedia-style website — modelled after Wikipedia in
        spirit but focused exclusively on medicine. Every article lives at a stable URL based on
        its file path, so linking between articles is predictable and durable. Articles are
        grouped into medical specialties (Cardiology, Neurology, Respiratory, Endocrinology,
        Gastroenterology) and the entire site is generated from Markdown and MDX files.
      </p>
      <p>
        The project is intentionally minimalist. There is no JavaScript framework on the client,
        no tracking, no comments and no advertisements. Pages render as plain HTML and CSS, with
        one tiny inline script that powers the live search box.
      </p>

      <h2>How articles are organized</h2>
      <p>
        Articles live under <code>src/content/articles/&lt;category&gt;/&lt;article-slug&gt;.md</code>.
        The first directory segment becomes the article's category; the file name becomes the
        article's slug. The URL is simply <code>/articles/&lt;category&gt;/&lt;article-slug&gt;</code>,
        and articles within each category are listed in alphabetic path order — the same order
        they appear in the sidebar.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each article's frontmatter declares its <code>title</code>, <code>description</code>,
        <code>category</code>, optional <code>synonyms</code>, <code>lastUpdated</code> date and
        optional <code>references</code>. Internal links use standard Markdown link syntax pointing
        at the article's path, e.g. <code>[hypertension](/articles/cardiology/hypertension)</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>How to add an article</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Create a new <code>.md</code> or <code>.mdx</code> file under the appropriate category folder.</li>
        <li>Add frontmatter with <code>title</code>, <code>description</code> and <code>category</code>.</li>
        <li>Write the body using Markdown. Link to other articles with their path.</li>
        <li>Run <code>npm run dev</code> to preview locally, or <code>npm run build</code> to produce a static site in <code>dist/</code>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Medical disclaimer</h2>
      <p>
        Articles on MedWiki are for general education only. They are not a substitute for
        professional medical advice, diagnosis or treatment. Always seek the advice of a qualified
        healthcare provider with any questions about a medical condition. Never disregard
        professional medical advice because of something you read here.
      </p>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
